= Black Walnut (Juglans nigra)

_Scope: This entry treats_ Juglans nigra _L. — the eastern North American black walnut — and principally its_ green hull (husk), _the fleshy outer fruit-wall that is the drug of Western herbalism (anthelmintic, antifungal, astringent, and dye); the_ leaf _and the edible_ nut (kernel) _are treated secondarily. The entry sharply distinguishes_ J. nigra _from the_ Old World Persian / English walnut Juglans regia _L. — the walnut of the classical traditions (Chinese 胡桃/核桃, Sanskrit अक्षोट akṣoṭa, Arabic جوز jawz) — and from the North American_ butternut / white walnut Juglans cinerea _L. Status:_ classical only in Native American and Western (Eclectic) herbal medicine; _New World and therefore_ absent from the Chinese, Ayurvedic, and Tibb canons, _which know only_ J. regia. _The Old-World-system sections below are reasoned extension — explicitly by analogy to_ J. regia _and flagged as non-classical for_ J. nigra.

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Juglans nigra_ L., family Juglandaceae; the drug of Western herbalism is the *green hull / husk* (the fleshy exocarp of the fruit), with the *leaf* and the *nut* as secondary. _Juglans_ is a contraction of *Jōvis glāns*, "Jupiter's / Jove's acorn (nut)" — the classical Latin conceit that the walnut was the nut of the gods, above the common acorn. _nigra_ = "black," for the dark heartwood and the near-black staining of the hull. Distinguish carefully: *_J. regia_* L. (Persian / English / "common" walnut — the Old-World classical species), and *_J. cinerea_* L. (butternut / white walnut — a second North American species with parallel but distinct medicinal use).#footnote[_Juglans_ < _Jōvis glāns_ ("Jupiter's nut"): Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _juglans_); de Vaan, _Etymological Dictionary of Latin_. Species distinctions (_J. nigra_ vs _J. regia_ vs _J. cinerea_): standard botanical authorities. The medicinal drug in Western herbalism is the green hull; the kernel is the food.]

*English:* _Walnut_ — from Old English *walhhnutu*, "foreign nut" (_wealh_, "foreigner, Celt, Roman/Romance-speaker" + _hnutu_, "nut") — the "*Roman / foreign nut*," so named to contrast the introduced Old-World walnut with the native hazelnut. _Black_ walnut specifies the dark-wooded North American _J. nigra_. (The _wealh_ element is the same one preserved in "Welsh" and "Cornwall.")#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "walnut, n."): OE _walhhnutu_ < _wealh_ "foreign" + _hnutu_ "nut," i.e. the "foreign/Roman nut" as opposed to the native hazel. The _wealh_ root also yields "Welsh," "Cornwall," "walnut," and "gaul."]

*Chinese:* _J. nigra_ is *not classical* — it is the American species. The classical Chinese walnut is *_J. regia_*: *胡桃 (hútáo, "Western/barbarian peach")* or *核桃 (hétáo)*, with the kernel *胡桃仁 / 核桃仁 (hútáo rén / hétáo rén)* as the _Bencao_ drug; the 胡 prefix marks the Silk-Road (Western-Regions) introduction. _J. nigra_ is a modern introduction, named *黑胡桃 (hēi hútáo, "black walnut")* — post-classical, flagged.#footnote[_J. regia_ as classical 胡桃/核桃 with kernel drug 核桃仁: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 胡桃 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The 胡 provenance-prefix is discussed in _garlic.typ_ and the glossary. _J. nigra_ (黑胡桃) is a modern American introduction with no classical entry.]

*Sanskrit:* _J. nigra_ is *not classical*. The classical Indian walnut is _J. regia_: *अक्षोट (akṣoṭa)* / *अक्षोड (akṣoḍa)*, also *कर्कट (karkaṭa)* — the brain-and-nerve tonic nut of Ayurveda; Hindi *अखरोट (akhrōṭ)*. _J. nigra_ has no Sanskrit name.#footnote[_Akṣoṭa_ (_J. regia_): Monier-Williams (s.v. _akṣoṭa_, _akṣoḍa_); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu. No classical Sanskrit name exists for the American _J. nigra_.]

*Arabic / Persian (Tibb):* _J. nigra_ is *not classical*. The classical walnut is _J. regia_: *جوز (jawz)*, *جوز شامي (jawz shāmī, "Levantine walnut")*; Persian *گردو (gerdū)*. The green hull of _J. regia_ is *قشر الجوز (qishr al-jawz)*, used in Tibb and folk medicine as astringent, anthelmintic, and dye — the closest classical analogue to the black-walnut-hull drug, applied here by extension. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.#footnote[_Jawz_ / _jawz shāmī_ (_J. regia_) and the hull _qishr al-jawz_: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II (_jawz_); Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_. No Tibb entry exists for the American _J. nigra_; the hull-drug parallel is drawn from _J. regia_'s _qishr al-jawz_.]

== Classical Native American & Western herbal tradition

_This is the substantive classical tradition for_ Juglans nigra, _exclusively — the plant is North American and post-Columbian to every Old-World system._

- *Native American use:* Eastern Woodlands peoples (Cherokee, Iroquois, Delaware, and others) used _J. nigra_ (and the related butternut _J. cinerea_) extensively — the *green hull* as an *anthelmintic* (intestinal worms) and *antifungal* wash (ringworm, "tetterworm," skin fungus), the *bark and hull* as a strong *brown-black dye* (the juglone stain), and the nut as a staple mast food. Butternut bark was a noted Native American and later Eclectic *laxative/cholagogue*.
- *Eclectic and Western herbalism:* the *green-hull tincture* is the classic Western vermifuge and antifungal — for pinworm and other intestinal parasites, and topically for ringworm, athlete's foot, and _Candida_ skin infection; the hull and leaf are also *astringent* (for diarrhea and skin). This is a genuine, documented Western-herbal tradition, distinct from the Old-World _J. regia_ literature.
- *Modern popular register (flagged):* black-walnut hull is the first ingredient of the popular "*parasite cleanse*" trio — black walnut + *wormwood* + *clove* — associated with Hulda Clark; this is a *modern, commercially popular, and scientifically unsubstantiated* protocol, noted here for completeness and explicitly flagged as fringe, not as classical or evidence-based. (A curiosity: all three herbs of that trio are separate entries in this materia medica.)#footnote[Native American and Eclectic use of _J. nigra_/_J. cinerea_ hull and bark (anthelmintic, antifungal, astringent, dye; butternut as laxative): Moerman, _Native American Ethnobotany_ (Timber Press, 1998); King's _American Dispensatory_ (Felter & Lloyd) for the Eclectic register. The "parasite cleanse" (black walnut + wormwood + clove) is a modern popular protocol (Hulda Clark) — flagged as unsubstantiated; it has no classical or robust clinical basis.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Juglans nigra has no classical Chinese entry_ — it is the American 黑胡桃. The reading below is *reasoned extension*, and it must be split from the genuinely classical _J. regia_ drug, which is a *different medicine*:

- *Classical _J. regia_ (胡桃仁, for contrast, not the subject):* warm (溫), sweet (甘); Kidney (腎), Lung (肺), Large Intestine (大腸); *補腎助陽* (tonifies Kidney, assists yang — for lumbar weakness, impotence), *溫肺定喘* (warms Lung, calms wheezing), *潤腸通便* (moistens the intestine). The doctrine-of-signatures "brain-shaped nut benefits the brain" belongs here.
- *Reasoned reading of _J. nigra_ green hull (the actual subject):* the *hull* is a different part with a different character — bitter (苦), astringent (澀), cool-to-neutral; reasoned actions *殺蟲 (shā chóng, kills worms)*, *燥濕止癢 (dries damp, stops itch — the antifungal skin use)*, *清熱解毒* and *收澀 (astringes)*. This is *reasoned, not canonical*, and is a hull-drug quite unlike the warming tonic kernel of 胡桃仁.#footnote[The classical _J. regia_ 核桃仁 profile (warm-sweet Kidney-Lung tonic): Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The _J. nigra_ green-hull reading (bitter-astringent anthelmintic/antifungal) is reasoned cross-system placement anchored on the Western-herbal and modern-pharmacology data, not a Bencao classification — flagged.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical, for_ Juglans nigra. _The classical_ akṣoṭa _is_ J. regia _(a nut treated as brain-and-nerve tonic); the American_ J. nigra _hull is a different part of a different species._

- *Classical _J. regia_ (akṣoṭa, for contrast):* madhura (sweet) rasa; guru (heavy), snigdha (unctuous) guṇa; uṣṇa (heating) vīrya; madhura vipāka; _vāta-hara_, _balya_ (strengthening), _vṛṣya_ (aphrodisiac), _medhya_ (nervine/intellect — the brain-nut doctrine), _brṃhaṇa_ (nourishing).
- *Reasoned reading of _J. nigra_ green hull (the subject):* Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent) and Tikta (तिक्त, bitter) rasa; Laghu (light), Rūkṣa (dry) guṇa; Uṣṇa or anuṣṇa (mildly heating) vīrya; Kaṭu vipāka. Reasoned karma: *krimighna* (anthelmintic — the core use), *kuṣṭhaghna* / *kaṇḍūghna* (antidermatosis / antipruritic — the antifungal skin use), _saṃgrāhī_ (astringent, antidiarrheal), *raktaśodhaka* (blood-purifier, in the folk-dermatological sense).

*Prabhāva:* none classically (no classical entry for _J. nigra_); if singled out, the *juglone-mediated antifungal/anthelmintic potency* of the hull is the candidate feature — modern interpretive, flagged.

*Doṣa effect (reasoned, hull):* Kapha-Pitta-śāmaka via the kaṣāya-tikta profile; Vāta-vardhaka in excess (rūkṣa). Note this is the *opposite* leaning to the classical _vāta-hara_ snigdha kernel — the two parts diverge.#footnote[Classical _akṣoṭa_ (_J. regia_) as snigdha-guru vāta-hara medhya brain-tonic: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II. The _J. nigra_ green-hull reading (astringent-bitter krimighna/kuṣṭhaghna) is reasoned cross-system placement, not classical — flagged; and it diverges in doṣa-leaning from the classical kernel.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical viruddha for J. nigra; extensions of conditional rules — tags S/N/F/M)_

- *(F/extended) Conditional:* Vāta / _rūkṣa_ excess (the drying-astringent hull); high-Pitta only via the mild uṣṇa edge.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Tree-nut allergy* — the *nut* (kernel) is a true tree nut with *anaphylaxis risk*; a hard contraindication for nut-allergic individuals.
  - *Juglone toxicity / cytotoxicity* — juglone is cytotoxic and mutagenic in some assays; the hull is for *short-course, low-dose* use, not chronic high dose; avoid in pregnancy and lactation (traditional caution plus juglone).
  - *Staining and GI irritation* — hull tincture strongly stains and, in excess, irritates the gut (tannin/juglone).
  - *Thyroid marketing (debunked)* — black walnut is popularly and wrongly marketed as an "iodine/thyroid" herb; flagged as unsupported.#footnote[Tree-nut anaphylaxis (the kernel): standard allergology. Juglone cytotoxicity/mutagenicity in assays: phytochemical/toxicology literature — verify current sources; supports short-course, low-dose use and pregnancy avoidance. The "iodine/thyroid" claim for black walnut is a popular-marketing overstatement without robust support — flagged.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Not classical in Tibb for_ Juglans nigra _— the American species was unknown to the medieval Arabic-Persian world. Tibb knows only the Persian walnut_ J. regia (جوز jawz), _whose green hull_ (قشر الجوز qishr al-jawz) _is the classical analogue used below by extension. See_ `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

- *Classical _J. regia_ (jawz, for contrast):* *Mizāj* Hot and Moist (the fresh nut) to Hot and Dry (the dried); *muqawwī al-dimāgh* (brain tonic), *muqawwī al-bāh* (aphrodisiac), *mughadhdhī* (nourishing), *muqawwī al-maʿida*. The *hull* _qishr al-jawz_ is *qābiḍ* (astringent), *dāfiʿ li-l-dīdān* (anthelmintic), a *ṣābigh* (dye) for hair (the classical black-hair dye), and *jālī* (cleansing) for the gums/teeth.
- *Reasoned _J. nigra_ placement:* on the hull axis, *Hot-Dry, 2nd degree* by extension; *qābiḍ*, *qātil/dāfiʿ li-l-dīdān* (anthelmintic — the American use), *jālī/muṭahhir* for fungal skin, *ṣābigh* (the juglone dye — matching _qishr al-jawz_'s hair-dye use). Entirely interpretive.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* the *walnut _J. regia_* has folk-Prophetic and dietetic attestation as a brain-and-body-strengthening nut; the *American _J. nigra_ has none* — flagged. No hull formula is asserted for _J. nigra_ from the canon.#footnote[_J. regia_ jawz and its hull _qishr al-jawz_ (astringent, anthelmintic, hair-dye): Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_. The _J. nigra_ Tibb reading is reasoned extension from _qishr al-jawz_, explicitly interpretive; no classical Tibb entry exists for the American species.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formula uses _J. nigra_. The classical _J. regia_ kernel (核桃仁) appears in Kidney-yang-and-Lung tonics (e.g. 青娥丸 _Qīng'é Wán_, with walnut, eucommia, and psoralea, for Kidney-vacuity lumbar pain) — noted only for contrast; the American hull-drug has no Chinese formulary.

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formula uses _J. nigra_. Classical _akṣoṭa_ (_J. regia_) appears in nervine/_medhya_ and _balya_ contexts and as a nut in _rasāyana_ diet — noted for contrast only.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and North American herbal

- *Greco-Roman (the _J. regia_ background):* the walnut of Dioscorides (_karyon basilikon_, "royal nut") and Pliny is _J. regia_; used as food, and the green hull/rind as astringent and anthelmintic and a hair-dye — the deep Old-World precedent for the same *hull* uses later found independently in North America.
- *North American herbal (the actual _J. nigra_ tradition):* *black-walnut green-hull tincture* — the classic Eclectic and folk *vermifuge and antifungal*, taken internally in small doses for intestinal parasites and applied topically for ringworm, athlete's foot, and skin fungus; *hull and leaf infusions* as astringent washes; the *hull as a permanent brown-black dye* (juglone). Butternut (_J. cinerea_) bark as a mild *laxative/cholagogue* in the same tradition.
- *Modern herbal-commercial:* standardized black-walnut-hull tinctures and capsules for "parasite/antifungal" use (evidence largely preclinical/traditional); the fringe "parasite cleanse" trio (flagged above).#footnote[Greco-Roman _J. regia_ (walnut and its astringent/anthelmintic/dye hull): Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ (Beck); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XV, XXIII. North American _J. nigra_/_J. cinerea_ herbal: Felter & Lloyd, _King's American Dispensatory_; Moerman, _Native American Ethnobotany_. Modern efficacy for the hull's antiparasitic/antifungal use is largely preclinical and traditional — flagged.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

No classical Tibb tradition for _J. nigra_; the classical _qishr al-jawz_ (_J. regia_ hull) preparations — astringent gargles, anthelmintic use, and the black hair-dye — are the analogues, covered above.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_J. nigra_ is a *cold-winter continental-temperate* tree of the eastern North American deciduous forest (roughly the Köppen Dfa/Dfb and humid-subtropical Cfa fringe), fruiting in autumn. It is not a tropical or Mediterranean plant, and — unlike the pan-Eurasian _J. regia_ — it has essentially *one native climatic home*. See `climates.typ`.

=== Temperate eastern North America (the sole native home)

Constitutionally a *cold-dry-to-cool-damp* autumn-and-winter zone (Vāta-Kapha; external 寒/燥 shading to 寒濕). *Traditional preparation and cares.* The *nut* is an autumn *mast food* — an energy-dense, warming, _snigdha_/_brṃhaṇa_ store laid up for winter, in the same constitutional logic as other temperate tree-nuts. The *green hull*, harvested in early autumn, is a *seasonal medicine-and-dye*, not a food: a short-course anthelmintic/antifungal and a textile/hair dye. The bitter-astringent hull and the rich oily kernel are *opposite constitutional tools from the same tree* — one drying-clearing, one nourishing-warming.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

_J. nigra_ is narrowly native, but its products travel: the *nut* is exported as a specialty baking ingredient, and the *hull tincture* is sold globally as a "parasite/antifungal" supplement far from its temperate home. The constitutional caution is not climatic (the anthelmintic *indication* is climate-neutral) but one of *form and dose*: chronic high-dose juglone-bearing hull extract, used outside the traditional short seasonal course, is a _mātrā_-mismatch, and the fringe long-"cleanse" protocols compound this. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Two very different products: the *nut (food)* and the *green hull (medicine/dye)*.

*The nut (kernel), per ~28 g (1 oz):* energy-dense (~170–190 kcal), high in fat with a meaningful *α-linolenic acid (ALA, omega-3)* fraction, protein, fiber, and minerals (magnesium, manganese, phosphorus, zinc); a distinctive, stronger flavor and much harder shell than _J. regia_. Nutritionally comparable to English walnut, with a somewhat different fatty-acid and polyphenol profile.#footnote[Nut composition: USDA FoodData Central (_Juglans nigra_, black walnut). Broadly comparable to _J. regia_ with a distinct fatty-acid/polyphenol profile; the harder shell and stronger flavor are characteristic.]

*The green hull / leaf — key constituents:*
- *Juglone (5-hydroxy-1,4-naphthoquinone)* — the characteristic naphthoquinone: the *allelopathic* agent (juglone suppresses many nearby plants — the famous "nothing grows under a black walnut"), the *brown-black dye/staining* compound, and the principal *antimicrobial/antifungal/anthelmintic* and cytotoxic principle.
- *Tannins* (astringency), flavonoids, and phenolic acids.#footnote[Juglone as the characteristic naphthoquinone (allelopathic, dye, antimicrobial, cytotoxic) plus hull tannins/flavonoids: phytochemical literature on _Juglans_ — verify current sources. Juglone allelopathy (inhibition of neighboring plants) is well documented.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Antifungal / antimicrobial* — juglone has robust in vitro activity (the ringworm/athlete's-foot/_Candida_ use); mechanism includes redox cycling and enzyme inhibition.
- *Anthelmintic / antiparasitic* — traditional and preclinical (the core hull use); clinical evidence is limited.
- *Astringent* — tannins (topical and antidiarrheal use).
- *Cytotoxic / antitumor (preclinical, double-edged)* — juglone is cytotoxic to various cell lines (some anticancer interest) *and* genotoxic in some assays — the same property underlies both the interest and the caution against chronic high dose.
- *Allelopathy* — of horticultural, not medicinal, note but constituent-defining.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* the limiting compound is *juglone* (and, for the nut, *allergy*).
- *Nut (food):* unrestricted *except* in tree-nut allergy, where it is a serious *anaphylaxis* risk.
- *Green-hull tincture (traditional medicine):* small doses, *short courses only* (days to a few weeks); no well-established formal upper limit exists — treat juglone's cytotoxicity/genotoxicity as the reason to keep dose low and duration short.
- *Contraindications / cautions:* *pregnancy and lactation* (traditional caution + juglone); tree-nut allergy (the nut); chronic high-dose use (juglone); GI irritation and staining; do not treat marketed "iodine/thyroid" claims as real.
- *Reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice* — traditional anthelmintic/antifungal use is real but weakly quantified; genuine parasitic infection warrants clinical diagnosis and treatment.#footnote[No formal ADI for juglone/black-walnut hull is well established; the cytotoxicity/genotoxicity data support short-course, low-dose use and pregnancy avoidance — verify against current toxicology literature. Tree-nut anaphylaxis: standard allergology. These are reference cautions, not medical advice; suspected parasitic disease should be clinically diagnosed and treated.]

